#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd375637-3108-500f-8097-17642306cf77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299967} {:answer/id #uuid "5420c23c-5ecf-560f-942e-5608c17f15fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299968} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4f6458-e56e-5323-b568-47c13f2821ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299969} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8a210c-9ffb-5c50-90b4-7ab6003828f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299970} {:answer/id #uuid "c02edf13-4929-508d-a4f4-aff39fd8b99d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd375637-3108-500f-8097-17642306cf77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299967} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb1c39e-68f2-5e93-811f-11788192bcb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "462643fb-436c-555b-b2ee-366872ee8da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-118117" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{{v}^{2}+4v}=\\frac{3}{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)}=\\frac{3}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(v+4,\\,v(v+4),\\,v)=v(v+4).\n$$\n\n- The values $v=0$ and $v=-4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)} & =\\frac{3}{v} \\\\ v(v+4)\\cdot (\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)}) & =v(v+4)\\cdot \\frac{3}{v} \\\\ \\frac{6v(v+4)}{v+4}+\\frac{12v(v+4)}{v(v+4)} & =\\frac{3v(v+4)}{v} \\\\ \\frac{6v\\cdot \\cancel{(v+4)}}{\\cancel{v+4}}+\\frac{12\\cdot \\cancel{v(v+4)}}{\\cancel{v(v+4)}} & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{v}\\cdot (v+4)}{\\cancel{v}} \\\\ 6v+12 & =3(v+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $v,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6v+12 & =3(v+4) \\\\ 6v+12 & =3v+12 \\\\ 3v & =0 \\\\ v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $v=0$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "718ce9f2-3910-59d2-b4e2-70d8f44e6179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299974} {:answer/id #uuid "d115331c-9902-58c7-b8f0-73e69deec0fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299975} {:answer/id #uuid "457453fb-d7b5-5ce5-b7d5-6e3e0246907d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299976} {:answer/id #uuid "eea54914-d422-5ed5-9460-5ecf87afa8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299977} {:answer/id #uuid "85f79d45-8a79-51a1-8352-9941a90016ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d115331c-9902-58c7-b8f0-73e69deec0fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299975} :answer-field/id #uuid "2be70a65-fd55-55f8-9cb8-a95c6ba91ebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0817ca7-cfa9-5867-a1b6-3a753c07c1ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-118173" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}-r}-\\frac{1}{r-1}=\\frac{1}{3r}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{1}{r-1}=\\frac{1}{3r}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(r(r-1),\\,r-1,\\,3r)=3r(r-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $r=0$ and $r=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{1}{r-1} & =\\frac{1}{3r} \\\\ 3r(r-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{1}{r-1}) & =3r(r-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3r} \\\\ \\frac{3r(r-1)}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{3r(r-1)}{r-1} & =\\frac{3r(r-1)}{3r} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{r(r-1)}}{\\cancel{r(r-1)}}-\\frac{3r\\cdot \\cancel{(r-1)}}{\\cancel{r-1}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{3r}\\cdot (r-1)}{\\cancel{3r}} \\\\ 3-3r & =r-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $r,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3r & =r-1 \\\\ 4 & =4r \\\\ r & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $r=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc48c81d-3e25-5ee4-8afb-9fe4334d47de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299981} {:answer/id #uuid "93cf3125-bb8f-5ea6-a044-625cb0d92e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc48c81d-3e25-5ee4-8afb-9fe4334d47de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299981} :answer-field/id #uuid "13157722-2b8d-53d6-8e6a-3d21c97a6776" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed713b7d-3a96-528c-9894-42c126960372" :question/math-academy-id "q-276461" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{R-1}-\\frac{2}{R-2}=\\frac{2}{{R}^{2}-3R+2}.$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{R-1}-\\frac{2}{R-2}=\\frac{2}{(R-1)(R-2)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(R-1,\\,R-2,\\,(R-1)(R-2))=(R-1)(R-2).\n$$\n\n- The values $R=1$ and $R=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{R-1}-\\frac{2}{R-2} & =\\frac{2}{(R-1)(R-2)} \\\\ (R-1)(R-2)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{R-1}-\\frac{2}{R-2}) & =(R-1)(R-2)\\cdot \\frac{2}{(R-1)(R-2)} \\\\ \\frac{3(R-1)(R-2)}{R-1}-\\frac{2(R-1)(R-2)}{R-2} & =\\frac{2(R-1)(R-2)}{(R-1)(R-2)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(R-1)}\\cdot 3(R-2)}{\\cancel{R-1}}-\\frac{2(R-1)\\cdot \\cancel{(R-2)}}{\\cancel{R-2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(R-1)(R-2)}}{\\cancel{(R-1)(R-2)}} \\\\ 3(R-2)-2(R-1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $R,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(R-2)-2(R-1) & =2 \\\\ 3R-6-2R+2 & =2 \\\\ R & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $6\\ne 1$ and $6\\ne 2,$ we conclude that $R=6$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $R=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fec0b583-bb85-5c1c-99c1-2696c4cdba64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fec0b583-bb85-5c1c-99c1-2696c4cdba64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299985} :answer-field/id #uuid "82e83541-a3be-5e19-8e01-3e843fabefe8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb8674d7-42b1-5627-8ccb-a330231217d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-276462" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{a-3}-\\frac{10}{{a}^{2}-2a-3}=\\frac{2}{a+1}.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{a-3}-\\frac{10}{(a+1)(a-3)}=\\frac{2}{a+1}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(a+1,\\,a-3,\\,(a+1)(a-3))=(a+1)(a-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $a=-1$ and $a=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{a-3}-\\frac{10}{(a+1)(a-3)} & =\\frac{2}{a+1} \\\\ (a+1)(a-3)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a-3}-\\frac{10}{(a+1)(a-3)}) & =(a+1)(a-3)\\cdot \\frac{2}{a+1} \\\\ \\frac{(a+1)(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{10(a+1)(a-3)}{(a+1)(a-3)} & =\\frac{2(a+1)(a-3)}{a+1} \\\\ \\frac{(a+1)\\cdot \\cancel{(a-3)}}{\\cancel{a-3}}-\\frac{10\\cdot \\cancel{(a+1)(a-3)}}{\\cancel{(a+1)(a-3)}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(a+1)}\\cdot (a-3)}{\\cancel{a+1}} \\\\ a+1-10 & =2(a-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $a,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+1-10 & =2(a-3) \\\\ a-9 & =2a-6 \\\\ a & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-3\\ne -1$ and $-3\\ne 3,$ we conclude that $a=-3$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $a=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9ca4787-4209-50b5-be76-313f3f7f2a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9ca4787-4209-50b5-be76-313f3f7f2a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299988} :answer-field/id #uuid "d565b952-6a12-5261-9d68-fc0951bf7629" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c2b3b50-b0c3-5c92-9e37-b911fab7e735" :question/math-academy-id "q-276466" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{7}{h}-\\frac{3}{2h-3}=\\frac{1}{2{h}^{2}-3h}.$\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{h}-\\frac{3}{2h-3}=\\frac{1}{h(2h-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(h,\\,2h-3,\\,h(2h-3))=h(2h-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $h=0$ and $h=\\frac{3}{2}$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{h}-\\frac{3}{2h-3} & =\\frac{1}{h(2h-3)} \\\\ h(2h-3)\\cdot (\\frac{7}{h}-\\frac{3}{2h-3}) & =h(2h-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{h(2h-3)} \\\\ \\frac{7h(2h-3)}{h}-\\frac{3h(2h-3)}{2h-3} & =\\frac{h(2h-3)}{h(2h-3)} \\\\ \\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{h}(2h-3)}{\\cancel{h}}-\\frac{3h\\cdot \\cancel{(2h-3)}}{\\cancel{2h-3}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{h(2h-3)}}{\\cancel{h(2h-3)}} \\\\ 7(2h-3)-3h & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $h,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(2h-3)-3h & =1 \\\\ 14h-21-3h & =1 \\\\ 11h & =22 \\\\ h & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\ne 0$ and $2\\ne \\frac{3}{2},$ we conclude that $h=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $h=2.$"}]}
